'''wallow'''<sup>1</sup> (wol′ō), ''n.'' [< ''wallow''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' The ac of rolling or tumbling, as in sand or mire.
{{smaller block|<poem>Wrothely thei wrythyne and wrystille to-gederz
With welters and ''walowes'' over with-in thase buskez.</poem>{{right|''Morte Arthure'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 114}}}}
'''2†.''' A rolling gait.
{{smaller block|<poem>One taught the toss, and one the new French ''wallow'';
His sword-knot this, his cravat that designed.</poem>{{right|''Dryden'', Epil. to Etherege’s Man of Mode}}}}
'''3.''' A place to which an animal, as a buffalo, resorts to wallow; also, the traces of its wallowing left in the mire. {{smaller|Some localities called by thi name (notably the “hog-wallows” of the San Joaqui Valley, in California) are on too large a scale to hav been formed in this way. Their origin has not been satisfactorily explained.}}

